# Source for q/ae-care3.js: Clinical care third batch (infection control, ergonomics, emergencies, ECG, meds, labs, communication).
C="Clinical care"
Q=[
# --- Infection control ---
(C,"The single most effective way to prevent spread of infection in the echo lab is:","Hand hygiene before and after every patient contact",["Wearing a mask all day","Cleaning only at the end of the day","Using gel only on the probe"],"Clean hands before touching the patient and after removing gloves.","F"),
(C,"Which infection requires washing hands with soap and water instead of alcohol rub?","Clostridioides difficile (C. diff)",["MRSA","Influenza","The common cold"],"Alcohol does not kill C. diff spores.","F"),
(C,"Which statement describes standard precautions?","Treating all blood and body fluids as potentially infectious for every patient",["Precautions only for known infections","Wearing a gown for every patient","Using no gloves"],"They form the base for all other isolation precautions.","F"),
(C,"A patient with active tuberculosis needs which precautions?","Airborne precautions with an N95 respirator and a negative-pressure room",["Contact precautions only","No precautions","Droplet precautions with a surgical mask only"],"Measles and chickenpox also need airborne precautions.","F"),
(C,"A patient with MRSA in a wound needs:","Contact precautions (gown and gloves)",["Airborne precautions","No precautions","Only a mask"],"Clean the machine and probe thoroughly afterward.","F"),
(C,"Influenza is spread mainly by large droplets, so the precautions are:","Droplet precautions with a surgical mask",["Airborne only","Contact only","None"],"Droplets travel about 1 to 2 meters.","F"),
(C,"The correct order for removing PPE is usually:","Gloves, then gown, then eye protection, then mask, then hand hygiene",["Mask first, then gloves","Gown first, then gloves, then wash hands","Any order"],"The most contaminated items come off first.","C"),
(C,"Which probes need high-level disinfection after each use?","Semi-critical probes that touch mucous membranes, such as TEE probes",["Only surface probes","No probes","Probes used on healthy skin only"],"Surface probes on intact skin need low-level disinfection.","F"),
(C,"Gel used for an exam on broken skin or during a sterile procedure should be:","Sterile, single-use gel",["Refilled from a large bottle","Warmed tap water","Any gel"],"Multi-use gel bottles can grow bacteria.","F"),
(C,"Patients in isolation are often scheduled:","At the end of the day, when possible, to reduce spread",["First thing in the morning","At random","Never scanned"],"The machine must be fully cleaned afterward.","F"),
(C,"A needlestick injury should be followed first by:","Washing the area with soap and water and reporting it right away",["Ignoring it","Squeezing and bandaging only","Waiting until the end of the shift"],"Early reporting allows testing and treatment if needed.","F"),
# --- Ergonomics and safety ---
(C,"Work-related musculoskeletal injury in sonographers most often affects the:","Shoulder, neck and wrist",["Knees only","Feet","Ears"],"Static postures and pressure cause strain.","F"),
(C,"To reduce shoulder strain, the scanning arm should be kept:","Close to the body, with abduction under about 30°",["Raised above the head","Fully stretched out","Behind the back"],"Raise or lower the bed to bring the patient closer.","F"),
(C,"Good ergonomic habits include:","Adjusting bed and chair height, keeping the wrist neutral and taking short breaks",["Gripping the probe tightly","Twisting the spine","Leaning over the patient"],"Use support cushions for the arm.","F"),
(C,"Before moving a patient in a wheelchair onto the bed, the sonographer should:","Lock the wheelchair brakes",["Push the chair quickly","Leave the footrests down","Lift the patient alone"],"Lock the bed wheels too.","F"),
(C,"Oxygen cylinders should be:","Secured upright in a holder, never left loose",["Laid on the floor","Placed near heat","Left unsecured"],"A falling cylinder can be very dangerous.","F"),
(C,"Before starting any exam, the patient must be identified using:","At least two identifiers, such as full name and date of birth",["The room number only","The bed label only","The referring doctor's name"],"This prevents exams and reports on the wrong patient.","F"),
# --- Emergencies ---
(C,"Which rhythms can be treated with a defibrillator shock?","Ventricular fibrillation and pulseless ventricular tachycardia",["Asystole","Pulseless electrical activity","Sinus rhythm"],"Asystole and PEA need CPR and drugs, not shocks.","F"),
(C,"The compression-to-breath ratio for adult CPR with one rescuer is:","30:2",["15:1","5:1","50:5"],"Push hard and fast, allowing full chest recoil.","F"),
(C,"When an AED arrives during a cardiac arrest, the rescuer should:","Turn it on and follow the voice prompts as soon as possible",["Wait 10 minutes first","Use it only after CPR stops for good","Read the manual first"],"Early defibrillation improves survival.","F"),
(C,"A patient feels faint, with a slow pulse and low BP, after seeing a needle. This is likely:","A vasovagal reaction; lay the patient flat and raise the legs",["A heart attack","A stroke","An allergic reaction"],"Most patients recover quickly lying down.","F"),
(C,"In a STEMI, the goal from arrival to opening the artery (door-to-balloon) is:","90 minutes or less",["6 hours","24 hours","10 hours"],"Time lost means more heart muscle lost.","F"),
(C,"If a patient develops sudden shortness of breath, chest pain and low oxygen during the exam, the sonographer should:","Stop, stay with the patient and call for help, then look for RV strain if asked",["Finish the full study first","Leave the patient alone","Tell the patient to go home"],"Possible pulmonary embolism is an emergency.","F"),
(C,"A Glasgow Coma Scale score of 8 or less means:","Severe reduction in consciousness",["Normal alertness","Mild confusion","Normal sleep"],"Patients may need airway protection.","C"),
(C,"Orthostatic hypotension is a drop in systolic BP of at least:","20 mmHg within about 3 minutes of standing",["5 mmHg","50 mmHg lying down","Any small drop"],"Patients may feel dizzy when getting up from the exam table.","F"),
(C,"A fever is usually defined as a temperature of at least:","38.0 °C (100.4 °F)",["36.5 °C","35 °C","40 °C only"],"Fever with a new murmur raises concern for endocarditis.","F"),
(C,"Pulse oximetry readings can be inaccurate with:","Cold hands, poor perfusion, nail polish or carbon monoxide poisoning",["Normal circulation","Good signal","Warm fingers"],"Check the waveform for a reliable signal.","F"),
(C,"A 'do not resuscitate' (DNR) order means:","CPR should not be started if the heart stops, but other care continues",["No care should be given","The echo cannot be done","The patient refuses all tests"],"Check the chart before an emergency arises.","F"),
# --- ECG ---
(C,"The correct color placement for standard 3-lead ECG (US colors) is:","White on the right arm, black on the left arm, red on the left leg",["Red on the right arm","Black on the right leg only","Any position"],"A saying: 'white on right, smoke (black) over fire (red).'","F"),
(C,"A normal QRS duration is under about:","120 ms (0.12 s)",["200 ms","40 ms","300 ms"],"A wide QRS suggests a bundle branch block or ventricular rhythm.","F"),
(C,"Peaked T waves on the ECG suggest:","High potassium (hyperkalemia)",["Low potassium","Low calcium only","Normal finding always"],"Severe hyperkalemia can cause dangerous arrhythmias.","F"),
(C,"Prominent U waves and flat T waves suggest:","Low potassium (hypokalemia)",["High potassium","High calcium","Normal finding"],"Low potassium raises the risk of arrhythmias.","C"),
(C,"Mobitz type I (Wenckebach) second-degree block shows:","A PR interval that gets longer until a beat is dropped",["A fixed PR with sudden dropped beats","No P waves","A very short PR"],"Mobitz II has a fixed PR with sudden dropped beats and is more dangerous.","F"),
(C,"Torsades de pointes is a form of:","Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia linked to a long QT",["Atrial flutter","Sinus tachycardia","Heart block"],"It is treated with magnesium and correcting the cause.","C"),
(C,"Supraventricular tachycardia (SVT) usually shows:","A regular narrow-complex tachycardia, often 150–250 bpm",["A wide irregular rhythm","No QRS complexes","A rate under 60"],"Vagal maneuvers or adenosine may stop it.","F"),
(C,"After a premature ventricular contraction (PVC), the next normal beat often has:","A stronger contraction and higher Doppler velocities",["Weaker contraction","No contraction","No change"],"Longer filling time increases stroke volume. Avoid measuring that beat.","F"),
(C,"Muscle tremor or patient movement on an ECG appears as:","An irregular, noisy baseline (artifact)",["Normal sinus rhythm","Peaked T waves","Heart block"],"Ask the patient to relax and check lead contact.","F"),
# --- Medications ---
(C,"Nitroglycerin should not be given to a patient who recently took:","Sildenafil or other PDE-5 inhibitors",["Aspirin","Paracetamol","Insulin"],"The combination can cause severe low blood pressure.","F"),
(C,"Digoxin toxicity may cause:","Nausea, visual changes (yellow vision) and arrhythmias",["High fever","Rash only","Hair loss"],"Low potassium raises the risk of toxicity.","C"),
(C,"Heparin therapy is usually monitored with:","aPTT (activated partial thromboplastin time) or anti-Xa level",["INR","Troponin","BNP"],"Warfarin is monitored with the INR.","F"),
(C,"Direct oral anticoagulants (DOACs) such as apixaban are NOT recommended for:","Patients with mechanical heart valves",["Nonvalvular atrial fibrillation","DVT treatment","Pulmonary embolism"],"Warfarin is still required for mechanical valves.","F"),
(C,"A common side effect of ACE inhibitors is:","Dry cough",["Weight gain","Hair growth","Hearing loss"],"ARBs are an alternative that rarely cause cough.","F"),
(C,"Amiodarone is used for:","Ventricular and atrial arrhythmias",["Lowering cholesterol","Treating infections","Thinning blood"],"Long-term use can affect the thyroid, lungs and liver.","F"),
(C,"Adenosine is given to:","Briefly block the AV node and stop many SVTs",["Raise blood pressure","Treat heart block","Dissolve clots"],"It can cause a short pause and flushing.","F"),
(C,"Patients with a known allergy to polyethylene glycol (PEG) should be checked carefully before receiving:","Some ultrasound enhancing agents that contain PEG",["Saline","Oxygen","Gel"],"Know the ingredients of the agent used in your lab.","C"),
(C,"Calcium channel blockers such as verapamil and diltiazem:","Slow heart rate and AV conduction",["Speed up the heart","Cause heart block reversal","Have no heart effects"],"They are used for rate control in AF.","F"),
(C,"SGLT2 inhibitors (such as empagliflozin), originally for diabetes, are now used to:","Treat heart failure",["Treat infections","Dissolve clots","Raise heart rate"],"They reduce hospital stays for heart failure.","C"),
# --- Labs ---
(C,"A normal serum sodium is about:","135–145 mEq/L",["100–110 mEq/L","3.5–5.0 mEq/L","200–250 mEq/L"],"Low sodium is common in advanced heart failure.","F"),
(C,"Normal adult hemoglobin is roughly:","12–16 g/dL in women and 13.5–17.5 g/dL in men",["3–5 g/dL","25–30 g/dL","50 g/dL"],"Anemia can cause a high-output state and flow murmurs.","F"),
(C,"Kidney function is commonly assessed with:","Creatinine and eGFR",["Troponin","BNP","INR"],"Kidney disease can matter for some contrast agents and medicines.","F"),
(C,"For suspected endocarditis, blood cultures are usually drawn as:","At least two or three sets from separate sites before antibiotics",["One set after antibiotics","A single drop","Not needed"],"Positive cultures are a major Duke criterion.","F"),
(C,"An elevated lactate level in a critically ill patient suggests:","Poor tissue perfusion (shock)",["Good perfusion","Kidney stones","Normal finding"],"Echo can help find the cause of shock.","F"),
(C,"High CRP and ESR suggest:","Inflammation, such as in pericarditis or infection",["Low cholesterol","Normal heart function","Dehydration only"],"They are not specific to one disease.","F"),
(C,"The 'bad' cholesterol linked most closely to coronary disease is:","LDL",["HDL","Triglycerides only","Vitamin D"],"Statins lower LDL.","F"),
# --- Communication and professionalism ---
(C,"When receiving a critical result by phone, the receiver should:","Write it down and read it back to confirm",["Remember it without writing","Ignore it until morning","Tell only the patient"],"Read-back reduces communication errors.","F"),
(C,"The 'teach-back' method means:","Asking the patient to explain the instructions in their own words",["Teaching the patient anatomy","Reading the report to the patient","Writing a summary only"],"It checks that the patient understood.","F"),
(C,"A patient under 18 (a minor) usually needs consent from:","A parent or legal guardian, except in emergencies",["The sonographer","Any adult in the waiting room","No one"],"Rules can vary by local law.","F"),
(C,"A chaperone during an exam is important when:","The patient requests one or the exam involves sensitive areas",["Never","Only for male sonographers","Only in children"],"Follow your facility policy and respect the patient's wishes.","F"),
(C,"An incident or near miss in the echo lab (such as a wrong-patient scan) should be:","Reported through the facility's safety reporting system",["Hidden","Ignored if no harm","Discussed only on social media"],"Reporting improves safety for everyone.","F"),
(C,"Sharing a patient's echo images on social media without consent is:","A breach of patient privacy (HIPAA) and professional ethics",["Acceptable if the face is not shown","Encouraged for teaching","Allowed for friends"],"Always remove identifiers and follow facility rules for teaching cases.","F"),
(C,"Echo lab accreditation in the US is commonly provided by the:","IAC (Intersocietal Accreditation Commission)",["FDA","OSHA","WHO"],"Accreditation reviews quality, staff credentials and protocols.","F"),
(C,"Maintaining an RDCS or RCS credential requires:","Continuing medical education (CME) credits",["Nothing after the exam","A new exam every month","A medical degree"],"Requirements are set by the credentialing body.","F"),
(C,"When a patient becomes angry about a long wait, the best response is to:","Listen calmly, acknowledge the frustration and explain what will happen next",["Argue back","Ignore the patient","Cancel the exam immediately"],"Calm, respectful communication helps the patient feel heard.","F"),
(C,"Pressure injuries during long exams can be prevented by:","Using padding and changing patient position when possible",["Leaving the patient still for hours","Using hard surfaces","Tightening straps"],"Elderly and immobile patients are at highest risk.","F"),
]
